  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nA band of showers from western to south central MN continues to\nprogress eastward this afternoon at about 20 kts. There have\nbeen a few isolated thunderstorms and brief heavy downpours\nembedded within the band, especially during the late morning and\nmidday. Intensity will continue waning as the afternoon \nprogresses and chances are low it will reach WI. South winds\nhave been gusty today and temperatures have once again reached\nthe 70s ahead of the band of rain where sun has been prevalent. \n\nA cold front over Montana today will propagate east tonight and\nreach the Upper Midwest Saturday. A warm and somewhat moist airmass\nwill keep lows quite mild tonight, likely remaining in the low \n60s. Temperatures will warm quickly ahead of the front which is\nexpected to be passing through western MN mid to late morning.\nHighs across southern/eastern MN and WI should reach the mid to\npossibly upper 70s before tumbling behind the front. Another\nband of showers will develop along the front throughout the day,\nthen weaken and scatter by evening across southern MN and WI. A\nfew showers may linger through Sunday. \n\nThe front will stall across the mid MS Valley Sunday, then lift\nback to the north Monday and Tuesday as an upper low over the\ndesert Southwest finally ejects northeast across the Plains.\nThis compact system will bring rain to the central Plains Sunday\nnight and the Upper Midwest Monday into early Tuesday. QPF is\npretty impressive along the track of the low from Nebraska to\neastern SD and western MN where amounts may exceed 1-1.5 \ninches. \n\nAnother system may follow quickly on the heels of the early \nweek system Tuesday night and Wednesday. There is still quite a\nbit of model spread after Tuesday, leading to continuing chances\nof showers and low confidence. Ridging will try to build back\nlate week and temperatures may trend back above normal for\nHalloween weekend.",
